# Dependencies

A dependency is new when a manifest gains it, including development and build
dependencies. A PR that adds one includes all of these:

1. **A reason.** Under "What and why", one line per new dependency
   giving its name, its licence, and why it's needed, for example
   `h5py (BSD-3-Clause): reads HDF5 checkpoint files, which the standard library can't`.
   If the approved plan doesn't name it, end the line with "(not in the plan)"
   and carry on; it doesn't stop the work.
2. **A committed lockfile.** Add the dependency with the package manager
   (`uv add`, `pnpm add`, `cargo add`), and commit the lockfile it writes in
   the same commit as the manifest. C and C++ have no lockfile, so pin an
   exact version instead: a vcpkg `builtin-baseline`, or a FetchContent
   `GIT_TAG` set to a full commit hash, or a `URL` with `URL_HASH`.
3. **An allowed licence.** Which licences are allowed depends on whether
   anything from the dependency ships:
   - **Shipped:** its code is copied into, linked into, or distributed with
     what the project ships, including runtime libraries. Its licence must be
     on the allow-list in `deny.toml` (`allow` under `[licenses]`), which
     covers every language.
   - **Development-only:** it runs only on developer and CI machines, and
     nothing from it is copied into, linked into, or shipped with the
     project. Formatters, linters, test runners, docs generators, and their
     own dependencies are examples. Its licence may be any OSI-approved
     licence, public domain (including CC0-1.0), a font licence such as
     OFL-1.1, or a Creative Commons licence on data, such as caniuse-lite's
     CC-BY-4.0 browser-support data. Under "What and why", list each licence
     off the allow-list, including a transitive dependency's, with the
     dependency that brings it in.

   Stop and ask under "Needs a person" before adding a shipped dependency whose
   licence isn't on the allow-list, or any dependency under AGPL, a
   source-available or non-commercial licence such as SSPL or BUSL, a custom
   licence, or no licence.

   | Language | Licence check |
   |---|---|
   | Rust | `mise run lint:rust` runs `cargo deny check licenses`, which fails on any crate whose licence isn't on the allow-list. It checks development dependencies too, so Rust holds them to the shipped list. |
   | Python, TypeScript, C and C++ | None exists. Check the licence by hand. |

   Never add to the allow-list to make a dependency pass.

Never edit a lockfile by hand. On a lockfile merge conflict, first check out
the base branch's lockfile, for example `git checkout origin/main -- uv.lock`,
because uv and Cargo refuse to read one with conflict markers. Then resolve
the manifest and rerun the lock command (`uv lock`, `pnpm install`, or
`cargo update --workspace`). This keeps the base branch's versions and adds
only what the merged manifest needs. Deleting the lockfile and locking from
scratch would instead move every dependency to the newest version its
manifest allows.
